{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 37, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a masterfully crafted film that delves into the origins of facebook and the turbulent relationships that shaped its rise. directed by david fincher with razor-sharp precision, the movie is less a biopic and more a psychological drama about ambition, betrayal, and the cost of innovation in the digital age. jesse eisenberg delivers a career-defining performance as mark zuckerberg, portraying him as brilliant, socially awkward, and emotionally detached — a genius who doesn’t understand human connection but builds a platform that redefines it.\n\nthe screenplay, written by aaron sorkin, is a tour de force of rapid-fire dialogue and intricate structure. the narrative unfolds through a dual courtroom framework, with the winklevoss twins and christopher kenny suing zuckerberg for theft of ideas, and the sandler brothers suing over equity. these legal proceedings serve as framing devices that allow sorkin to weave flashbacks into a compelling, non-linear tale that never feels disjointed. every line crackles with intelligence, wit, and subtext, turning legal jargon into gripping cinema.\n\njoseph gordon-levitt and armie hammer give standout performances as the winklevoss twins, with hammer’s dual role being a technical marvel that adds both humor and pathos. andy serkis, as the enigmatic sean parker, brings a chaotic, magnetic energy that embodies the excess and allure of early tech culture. his introduction at a party, surrounded by naked women and champagne, is one of the film’s most iconic moments — a perfect metaphor for the seductive, morally ambiguous world of startups.\n\nthe cinematography by jeff cronenweth is cool and restrained, using a muted palette that mirrors the emotional coldness of the characters. the score by trent reznor and atticus ross is haunting and electronic, perfectly capturing the isolation and urgency of the digital frontier. it’s not just background music — it’s a character in itself, pulsing with the heartbeat of a new era.\n\nwhat makes the social network truly remarkable is its refusal to offer easy answers. is zuckerberg a villain or a visionary? is facebook a triumph of innovation or a monument to exploitation? the film leaves these questions unresolved, forcing the audience to sit with the ambiguity. it’s not about right or wrong — it’s about consequences.\n\nin a time when social media dominates our lives, the social network feels more relevant than ever. it’s a cautionary tale wrapped in a thrilling narrative, a modern tragedy about connection lost in the pursuit of connection. it’s not just a movie about a website — it’s a movie about what we’ve become. flawless in execution, devastating in impact, and utterly unforgettable.", "logprobs": [{"token": "the", "logprob": -0.00014733182615600526}, {"token": " social", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": " network", "logprob": -9.536738616588991e-07}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -3.3378546504536644e-06}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003810394846368581}, {"token": " master", "logprob": -0.32646167278289795}, {"token": "fully", "logprob": -0.22785787284374237}, {"token": " crafted", "logprob": -0.0006224363460205495}, {"token": " film", "logprob": -0.003422595327720046}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -6.437094270950183e-05}, {"token": " del", "logprob": -1.823462724685669}, {"token": "ves", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " into", "logprob": -0.009775268845260143}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " origins", "logprob": -0.5621328353881836}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -4.410646579344757e-05}, {"token": " facebook", "logprob": -0.00022492263815365732}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7064450979232788}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -1.168244216387393e-05}, {"token": " turbulent", "logprob": -1.2295734882354736}, {"token": " relationships", "logprob": -0.44